{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Exp","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.GeomSum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.rate_sum_pinned","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.rate_sum_pinned","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/ChannelRangeGrowthClass.lean#L122-L132","doc":"**The p-p and n-n decay rates sum to a constant.** Given the pinned product\n`b_pp·b_nn = 1/t²` (here `t = [2]_q`), the two rates `L = -log b` satisfy\n`L_pp + L_nn = 2 log t`. Three rates, one constraint, one free parameter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.no_linear_bound_on_pairs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.no_linear_bound_on_pairs","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/ChannelRangeGrowthClass.lean#L110-L120","doc":"**And that is a different growth class from any extensive law.** No constant\nmultiple of `n` bounds the pair count, so a massless channel cannot be\nrepresented by a bounded per-site sum however the bond letters are chosen. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.massless_pair_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.massless_pair_sum","kind":"theorem","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/ChannelRangeGrowthClass.lean#L104-L108","doc":"**At zero decay every pair contributes fully.** A massless mediator weights\nall `n(n-1)/2` pairs by `exp(0) = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.tree_threshold_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.tree_threshold_iff","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/ChannelRangeGrowthClass.lean#L83-L102","doc":"**The tree criterion.** For a bounded-degree graph the shell at distance `k`\nholds at most `d(d-1)^{k-1}` sites, so a Yukawa weight `exp(-μk)` gives a\nbounded per-site sum exactly when `μ > log(d-1)`.\n\nThis is the WORST case (a tree). A nuclear packing has polynomial shell growth\nand needs no such threshold — see the module docstring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.geometric_partial_sum_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.ChannelRangeGrowthClass.geometric_partial_sum_le","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/ChannelRangeGrowthClass.lean#L68-L81","doc":"A geometric shell sum is bounded independently of how many shells are taken,\nexactly when the decay beats the growth ratio. This is what \"extensive\" means\nhere: a per-site bound that does not grow with the system. ","signatureChunk":0}]}